! VMS-style help library for LSE: HELP PASCAL [topic [subtopic]]
! "n NAME" starts a topic at level n; other lines are its text.
1 PASCAL
 Pascal is a small, readable programming language, designed in 1970 by
 Niklaus Wirth for teaching. A Pascal program says first what it works
 with (its variables) and then what it does (its statements).

 Pick a topic below. Each one is short and has examples you can copy.
 Inside LSE you rarely type a whole statement: type a word such as IF
 and press Tab, and LSE writes the template for you to fill in.

2 PROGRAM
 Every program has the same shape: a heading, declarations, and the
 statements between BEGIN and END. The full stop after the last END
 marks the end of the program.

   PROGRAM Hello(INPUT, OUTPUT);
   BEGIN
     WRITELN('Hello, world!')
   END.

 INPUT and OUTPUT say that the program reads the keyboard and writes to
 the screen. Statements are separated by semicolons; there is no ;
 before END (Pascal allows one there, but it is not needed).

 In LSE: a new .PAS file starts as %{compilation_unit}%. Press Tab and
 Enter to choose PROGRAM, then type the name.
3 Example
   PROGRAM Greet(INPUT, OUTPUT);
   VAR
     age : INTEGER;
   BEGIN
     WRITE('How old are you? ');
     READLN(age);
     WRITELN('Next year you will be ', age + 1:1)
   END.

2 VAR
 A variable is a named box that holds one value. You must declare
 every variable, with its type, in a VAR section before BEGIN:

   VAR
     secret, guess, tries : INTEGER;
     answer : CHAR;
     done : BOOLEAN;

 Several names can share a type: separate them with commas. A new
 variable holds no value until you give it one with := (LSE stops the
 program with %PAS-F-UNINITVAR if you use it before that).

 Names start with a letter and contain letters, digits and _. Pascal
 does not care about upper or lower case: Tries and TRIES are the same.
3 Example
   VAR
     count : INTEGER;
   BEGIN
     count := 0;
     count := count + 1;
     WRITELN(count:1)       { prints 1 }
   END.

2 TYPES
 The type of a variable says what kind of value it can hold.

   INTEGER   whole numbers: 42, -7, 0
   REAL      numbers with a fraction: 3.14, -0.5, 1.0E6
   BOOLEAN   TRUE or FALSE
   CHAR      one character: 'A', '7', ' '

 You can also make your own types in a TYPE section: ranges, arrays
 and records. See the subtopics.
3 Subranges
 A subrange allows only the values between two limits:

   TYPE
     Percent = 0..100;
     Digit = '0'..'9';
   VAR
     score : Percent;
3 ARRAY
 An array is a row of boxes of the same type, numbered by an index:

   VAR
     scores : ARRAY [1..10] OF INTEGER;
     i : INTEGER;
   BEGIN
     FOR i := 1 TO 10 DO
       scores[i] := 0;
     scores[3] := 95
   END.

 A PACKED ARRAY [1..n] OF CHAR holds a piece of text of length n.
3 RECORD
 A record keeps several named fields together; they can have different
 types. Write record.field to use one field.

   TYPE
     Player = RECORD
       name : PACKED ARRAY [1..10] OF CHAR;
       score : INTEGER
     END;
   VAR
     p : Player;
   BEGIN
     p.score := 10
   END.

2 ASSIGNMENT
 := puts a value into a variable. Read it as "becomes":

   tries := 0;              { tries becomes 0 }
   tries := tries + 1;      { tries becomes one more than it was }
   secret := RANDOM(100) + 1;

 The right-hand side is worked out first, then stored on the left. The
 value must fit the variable's type: you cannot put 3.5 in an INTEGER.
 Do not confuse := (store) with = (compare, as in IF guess = secret).

2 EXPRESSIONS
 An expression works out a value.

   + - *        add, subtract, multiply
   /            divide, giving a REAL (7 / 2 is 3.5)
   DIV  MOD     whole-number divide and remainder (7 DIV 2 is 3, 7 MOD 2 is 1)
   = <> < > <= >=   compare, giving TRUE or FALSE
   AND  OR  NOT     combine tests

 Put brackets round each comparison when you combine them:

   IF (guess > 0) AND (guess <= 100) THEN ...

 Standard functions: ABS SQR SQRT ODD ORD CHR SUCC PRED TRUNC ROUND,
 and RANDOM(n) (see RANDOM).

2 WRITELN
 WRITELN prints its items on the screen and then starts a new line.
 WRITE does the same but stays on the line (good for a question).

   WRITELN('Hello');                  { text goes in single quotes }
   WRITELN('You got it in ', tries:1, ' tries.');
   WRITE('Your guess? ');

 Separate several items with commas. A number is printed in a field of
 10 characters (REAL: 12) unless you give a width: tries:1 prints it
 with no spaces in front, x:8:2 prints a REAL with 2 decimals.
 WRITELN on its own prints an empty line. To print a quote, double it:
 WRITELN('It''s done').
3 Example
   WRITELN('Name':10, 'Score':6);
   WRITELN('Ada':10, 95:6);
   WRITELN('Area: ', 3.14159 * 2 * 2:6:2)

2 READLN
 READLN waits for the user to type a line and press RETURN, and puts
 what they typed into the variables in brackets:

   WRITE('Your guess? ');
   READLN(guess);

 If the user types letters where a number is expected, the program
 says %PAS-W-INVSYNINT, explains, and asks again, so it does not crash.
 READ reads values but stays on the same input line.
3 Example
   VAR
     a, b : INTEGER;
   BEGIN
     WRITE('Two numbers: ');
     READLN(a, b);
     WRITELN('Their sum is ', a + b:1)
   END.

2 IF
 IF chooses: it runs a statement only when a test is TRUE. ELSE gives
 the statement to run when the test is FALSE.

   IF guess < secret THEN
     WRITELN('Too low!')
   ELSE IF guess > secret THEN
     WRITELN('Too high!')
   ELSE
     WRITELN('Correct!')

 There is no semicolon just before ELSE: the IF is not finished yet.
 To run several statements, group them with BEGIN ... END:

   IF tries > 10 THEN
   BEGIN
     WRITELN('Out of tries!');
     WRITELN('The number was ', secret:1)
   END

 In LSE: type IF and press Tab, then Tab through the placeholders.
 %[else_part]% offers ELSE or ELSE IF; Ctrl-K erases it.

2 CASE
 CASE picks one of several statements by a value:

   CASE answer OF
     'Y', 'y' : WRITELN('Again!');
     'N', 'n' : WRITELN('Goodbye')
   OTHERWISE
     WRITELN('Please type Y or N')
   END

 The value can be an INTEGER, a CHAR or a BOOLEAN. Ranges such as
 1..5 are allowed as labels.

2 LOOPS
 A loop runs statements again and again. Pascal has three:

   WHILE test DO statement           test first, may run 0 times
   REPEAT statements UNTIL test      test last, runs at least once
   FOR i := 1 TO 10 DO statement     counts a fixed number of times

 A loop that never ends can be stopped with Ctrl-C while it runs.
3 WHILE
 WHILE runs its statement as long as the test is TRUE. The test is made
 first, so if it is FALSE at the start the statement never runs. Use
 BEGIN ... END to repeat several statements.

   n := 1;
   WHILE n <= 5 DO
   BEGIN
     WRITELN(n:1);
     n := n + 1
   END
3 REPEAT
 REPEAT runs the statements between REPEAT and UNTIL, then tests. It
 stops when the test is TRUE, so the statements always run at least
 once. No BEGIN ... END is needed.

   REPEAT
     WRITE('Your guess? ');
     READLN(guess)
   UNTIL guess = secret
3 FOR
 FOR counts: the control variable goes from the first value to the last,
 one step at a time (DOWNTO counts down).

   FOR i := 1 TO 10 DO
     WRITELN(i:2, ' squared is ', i * i:3);

   FOR i := 10 DOWNTO 1 DO
     WRITE(i:1, ' ');

 The control variable must be declared (INTEGER or CHAR) and should not
 be changed inside the loop.

2 PROCEDURES
 A procedure is a named group of statements. Declare it before the
 BEGIN of the program, then run it by writing its name. Procedures keep
 a program tidy: write the steps once, use them often.

   PROCEDURE SayHello;
   BEGIN
     WRITELN('Hello!')
   END;

   BEGIN
     SayHello;
     SayHello
   END.

 In LSE: on %[declarations]%, choose PROCEDURE (or type PROCEDURE and
 press Tab).
3 PARAMETERS
 Parameters pass values into a procedure. A value parameter gets a copy;
 a VAR parameter works on the caller's own variable and can change it.

   PROCEDURE ShowScore(name : CHAR; points : INTEGER);
   BEGIN
     WRITELN(name, ': ', points:1)
   END;

   PROCEDURE AddOne(VAR count : INTEGER);
   BEGIN
     count := count + 1
   END;
3 FUNCTIONS
 A function is a routine that gives back a value. Inside it, assign the
 result to the function's own name.

   FUNCTION Square(x : INTEGER) : INTEGER;
   BEGIN
     Square := x * x
   END;

   ...
   WRITELN(Square(7):1)      { prints 49 }

 Functions and procedures can call themselves (recursion): see
 examples/FACTORIAL.PAS.

2 RANDOM
 RANDOM(n) gives a whole number from 0 to n - 1, so RANDOM(100) + 1 is
 1 to 100. Call RANDOMIZE once at the start so that each run picks
 different numbers.

   RANDOMIZE;
   secret := RANDOM(100) + 1;

 RANDOMIZE and RANDOM come from the run-time library PASRTL (routine
 PAS$RANDOM); LINK adds them to your program. RUN/SEED=7 (or lse --seed 7)
 makes the numbers the same every time, which helps when testing.

2 COMMENTS
 A comment is a note for people; the compiler skips it. Write it in
 curly brackets or between (* and *):

   tries := 0;   { nobody has guessed yet }
   (* This also is a comment. *)

 That is why LSE's placeholders look like %{this}% and not {this}.

2 COMPILE-LINK-RUN
 Turning your program into something the computer runs takes three
 steps. In LSE, F5 does all three; F7 only compiles.

   COMPILE   checks the program and translates it into p-code:
             GUESS.PAS -> GUESS.OBJ (and GUESS.DIA, the messages)
   LINK      joins the object file with the ready-made routines it uses
             from the run-time library PASRTL.OLB:
             GUESS.OBJ -> GUESS.EXE (and GUESS.MAP, a list of the parts)
   RUN       starts GUESS.EXE on the p-code machine

 The status line shows where you are: EDIT > COMPILE > LINK > RUN. A
 stage marked * is out of date (you changed the program since), one
 marked ! failed.

 Outside LSE, in a shell:  pascal GUESS,  link GUESS,  run GUESS.
3 COMPILE
 COMPILE (F7) checks the whole program. Each mistake is reported as a
 message with an explanation and a hint; F8 goes to the next one and
 Shift-F8 to the previous one. REVIEW lists them all in a window.

   %PASCAL-E-SEMIEXP, ";" expected at line 9, column 3

 A compile with errors does not write GUESS.OBJ. COMPILE /LIST also
 writes a listing, GUESS.LIS.
3 LINK
 LINK puts the pieces together: your object file, any modules you
 compiled separately, and the run-time library. It writes GUESS.EXE and
 a map, GUESS.MAP, that lists every routine and where it came from. Open
 the map with Ctrl-O GUESS.MAP to see PAS$RANDOM taken from PASRTL.

 If a name is used but defined nowhere, LINK says %LINK-W-UNDFSYMS and
 does not write the .EXE.
3 RUN
 RUN (F5) gives the screen to your program: type your answers and press
 RETURN. Ctrl-C stops it. Afterwards, press RETURN to go back to LSE;
 what the program printed is in the buffer $OUTPUT (Ctrl-B lists buffers).

 If the program fails, for example by dividing by zero, it stops with a
 %PAS-F- message and a traceback listing the routines that were running
 and their line numbers. F8 goes to the line where it stopped.
3 MODULES
 A MODULE holds procedures and functions for other programs to use. Mark
 them [GLOBAL]; the program that uses them declares them [EXTERNAL].
 Compile each file, then LINK them together:

   LINK MATHDEMO, MATHLIB

 See examples/MATHLIB.PAS and examples/MATHDEMO.PAS.

2 MESSAGES
 Every message from the compiler, the linker and the running program
 looks like this:

   %PASCAL-E-UNDECLID, undeclared identifier "count" at line 12

 PASCAL (or LINK, PAS) says who is talking, the letter is the severity
 (I = information, W = warning, E = error, F = fatal) and UNDECLID is the
 message's name. Each comes with an explanation and a hint; SET MESSAGES
 /NOEXPLAIN leaves those out. Pick a message below to read about it.

2 PLACEHOLDERS
 LSE writes templates for you. The blanks in them are placeholders:

   %{name}%      required: Pascal needs something here
   %[name]%      optional: erase it with Ctrl-K if you do not need it
   %{name}%...   a list: after you fill one in, Tab starts the next

 Tab on a placeholder expands it or opens a menu of choices; typing
 replaces it; Shift-Tab goes back. Type a word such as IF, WHILE or
 WRITELN and press Tab to get its template. F1 on a placeholder explains
 it, with an example. A compile stops at any placeholder that is left
 (%PASCAL-E-PLACEHOLDER), and F8 takes you to it.
